OR PRACTICAL – GAME THEORY 1
Practical No. 5
 
Title
To solve a two-person zero-sum game using POM-QM Software and determine the optimal strategies and value of the game.
POM-QM (Production and Operations Management – Quantitative Methods) is a decision-support software used to solve Operations Research problems.
 
Aim
To apply Game Theory 1 for solving a two-person zero-sum game and to determine:
•	Optimal strategies of both players
•	Existence of a saddle point
•	Value of the game using POM-QM software
 
Software Used
POM-QM for Windows
 
Theory
Game Theory is a branch of Operations Research that studies decision-making situations involving two or more players with conflicting objectives.
In a two-person zero-sum game:
•	Gain of one player equals the loss of the other
•	Player A aims to maximize the payoff
•	Player B aims to minimize the payoff
•	Payoff matrix represents gains of Player A
 
Explanation of Saddle Point
A saddle point is a position in the payoff matrix where:
•	The maximum of the row minimums (Maximin)
is equal to
•	The minimum of the column maximums (Minimax)
If:
Maximin = Minimax
then the game has a saddle point and can be solved using a pure strategy.
If:
Maximin ≠ Minimax
then no saddle point exists, and the game must be solved using mixed strategies.
 
Problem Statement
Solve the following game and determine the optimal strategies and value of the game.
Player A \ Player B	B1	B2
A1	2	4
A2	3	1
 
Saddle Point Check
•	Row minimums:
o	A1 → min(2, 4) = 2
o	A2 → min(3, 1) = 1
→ Maximin = 2
•	Column maximums:
o	B1 → max(2, 3) = 3
o	B2 → max(4, 1) = 4
→ Minimax = 3
Since Maximin ≠ Minimax, no saddle point exists.
 
Procedure (Using POM-QM Software)
1.	Open POM-QM for Windows
2.	Select Module → Game Theory
3.	Click New
4.	Enter:
o	Player A strategies = 2
o	Player B strategies = 2
5.	Enter payoff values in the matrix
6.	Click Solve
7.	Note the optimal strategies and value of the game
 
Result / Output
•	Optimal strategy of Player A:
o	A1 = 0.5
o	A2 = 0.5
•	Optimal strategy of Player B:
o	B1 = 0.75
o	B2 = 0.25
•	Value of the Game:
o	V = 2.5
 
Conclusion
The given game has no saddle point, hence it is solved using mixed strategies.
POM-QM software provides the optimal strategies and the value of the game as 2.5.
 
Learning Outcome
•	Understood the concept of saddle point
•	Learned to identify pure and mixed strategy games
•	Gained hands-on experience with POM-QM software
