OR PRACTICAL – GAME THEORY–II
Practical No. 6
 
Title
To solve a two-person zero-sum game using mixed strategies and determine the optimal strategies and value of the game using POM-QM Software.
 
Aim
To apply Game Theory–II concepts for solving a two-person zero-sum game involving mixed strategies and to determine:
•	Optimal strategies of both players
•	Value of the game
using POM-QM software
 
Software Used
POM-QM (Production and Operations Management – Quantitative Methods) for Windows
 
Theory
Game Theory–II deals with games without a saddle point, where players cannot rely on pure strategies.
In such cases, players adopt mixed strategies, i.e., they play each strategy with a certain probability.
In a two-person zero-sum game:
•	One player’s gain is equal to the other player’s loss
•	Player A aims to maximize the payoff
•	Player B aims to minimize the payoff
•	Solution is obtained using probability distribution of strategies
 
Explanation of Mixed Strategy
A mixed strategy is a situation where a player does not choose a single strategy every time but assigns probabilities to each available strategy.
Mixed strategies are used when:
•	No saddle point exists
•	Maximin value ≠ Minimax value
 
Problem Statement
Solve the following game and find the optimal mixed strategies and value of the game.
Player A \ Player B	B1	B2
A1	1	5
A2	4	2
 
Saddle Point Check
•	Row minimums:
o	A1 → min(1, 5) = 1
o	A2 → min(4, 2) = 2
→ Maximin = 2
•	Column maximums:
o	B1 → max(1, 4) = 4
o	B2 → max(5, 2) = 5
→ Minimax = 4
Since Maximin ≠ Minimax, no saddle point exists.
Hence, the game is solved using mixed strategies.
 
Procedure (Using POM-QM Software)
1.	Open POM-QM for Windows
2.	Select Module → Game Theory
3.	Click on New
4.	Enter:
o	Number of strategies for Player A = 2
o	Number of strategies for Player B = 2
5.	Enter the payoff matrix values
6.	Click Solve
7.	Note the optimal strategies and value of the game
 
Result / Output
•	Optimal strategy of Player A:
o	A1 = 0.5
o	A2 = 0.5
•	Optimal strategy of Player B:
o	B1 = 0.5
o	B2 = 0.5
•	Value of the Game:
o	V = 3
 
Conclusion
The given game has no saddle point and hence is solved using mixed strategies.
The optimal strategies and value of the game are successfully obtained using POM-QM software.
 
Learning Outcome
•	Understood the application of mixed strategy games
•	Learned to identify games without saddle point
•	Gained practical exposure to POM-QM software

