📘 OR Practical No. 8
Decision Theory – II : Decisions Under Risk
 
🎯 AIM
To study decision-making under Risk using Decision Theory and to obtain the optimal decision with the help of POM-QM software.
 
📖 INTRODUCTION / THEORY
Decision Under Risk is a situation where:
•	Possible states of nature are known
•	Probabilities are known or can be estimated
•	Decision is made using expected values
👉 Main techniques:
•	Expected Monetary Value (EMV)
•	Expected Opportunity Loss (EOL)
•	Expected Value of Perfect Information (EVPI)
 
🔑 IMPORTANT TERMS
Term	Meaning
Alternatives	Available decisions
States of Nature	Future events
Probability	Likelihood of each state
Payoff	Profit / loss
EMV	Expected Monetary Value
 
📊 EXAMPLE
A company plans to launch a new product and must choose advertising strategy.
👉 Alternatives
•	A1: Low Advertisement
•	A2: Medium Advertisement
•	A3: High Advertisement
👉 States of Nature
•	S1: Low Demand (P = 0.3)
•	S2: Medium Demand (P = 0.5)
•	S3: High Demand (P = 0.2)
 
📈 Payoff Table (₹ in lakhs)
Decision	Low	Medium	High
Low Ad	4	6	8
Medium Ad	2	7	12
High Ad	-1	5	15
 
✍️ CALCULATIONS
1️⃣ Expected Monetary Value (EMV)
[
EMV = \sum (Payoff × Probability)
]
EMV Calculations:
•	Low Ad
= (4×0.3) + (6×0.5) + (8×0.2)
= 5.8
•	Medium Ad
= (2×0.3) + (7×0.5) + (12×0.2)
= 6.5
•	High Ad
= (-1×0.3) + (5×0.5) + (15×0.2)
= 5.2
✅ Optimal Decision (EMV): Medium Advertisement
 
2️⃣ Expected Opportunity Loss (EOL)
Minimum EOL gives the best decision.
Decision	EOL
Low Ad	1.7
Medium Ad	1.0
High Ad	2.1
✅ Decision: Medium Advertisement
 
3️⃣ Expected Value of Perfect Information (EVPI)
[
EVPI = Expected Payoff with Perfect Information – Max EMV
]
•	EV with PI = 7.5
•	Max EMV = 6.5
[
EVPI = 1.0
]
 
